13194139534731 true] [17592186078517 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306176 13194139534731 true] [17592186078517 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306179 13194139534731 true] [17592186078518 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306183 13194139534731 true] [17592186078518 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306191 13194139534731 true] [17592186078518 1020 #edb/ref 17592186306198 13194139534731 true] [17592186078946 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534731 true] [17592186078946 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/83b23d8138091a931d14854b197752d25d8149ee8914a70b8db73b6c2369c888.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534731 true] [17592186078946 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/626/Source/Images/q-49176.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y = f(x)$ above, find the value of\n$∫_{-2}^{5}f(x)dx$." 13194139534731 false] [17592186078946 1145 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-2,3)$ and the arc of the semi-circle for $x\\in [3,5].$ So, we can use the additive property of integrals to get\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-2}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,3).$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =b\\cdot h \\\\  & =5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [3,5].$ The corresponding region is a semi-circle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot (1)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =10+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534731 true] [17592186289658 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306187 13194139534731 true] [17592186296668 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/d43fa0efcfb8788316c86958b1175741c136588e81a8119131d1ca0f8d8ee229.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534731 false] [17592186296668 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/d43fa0efcfb8788316c86958b1175741c136588e81a8119131d1ca0f8d8ee229.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of four line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534731 true] [17592186296695 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14036389/assets/877771be920838a330edabe84b07eb817f1f3d7f1343b6866b9c2f078e079a67.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534731 false] [17592186296695 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/877771be920838a330edabe84b07eb817f1f3d7f1343b6866b9c2f078e079a67.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306158 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306159 13194139534731 true] [17592186306158 1089 "q-117680" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306158 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534731 true] [17592186306158 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/a433e9e9f2018c1f964f13b0ef248f72253f99f071f44e029c928598e9d04a32.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306158 1113 #uuid "042f12f3-e4d4-5dee-9b70-77f357143515" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306158 1145 "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,0)$ and the line segment for $x\\in [0,2].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={{{\\int }_{-4}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,0).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(4\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =8+4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306160 13194139534731 true] [17592186306159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306161 13194139534731 true] [17592186306159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306162 13194139534731 true] [17592186306159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306163 13194139534731 true] [17592186306159 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306164 13194139534731 true] [17592186306159 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534731 true] [17592186306159 1041 "selection" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306159 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306160 13194139534731 true] [17592186306159 1110 #uuid "98cde449-95b7-5409-84ce-9958ec24578e" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306160 1034 "12" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306160 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306160 1111 #uuid "1c8afb12-c143-5d6c-9273-ac8abd85f79d" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306161 1034 "8" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306161 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306161 1111 #uuid "65edb11e-1e0e-5d4b-bc4d-5dbf5cafa493" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306162 1034 "6" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306162 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306162 1111 #uuid "9c2c7d95-276c-51d5-8e6e-1785b9a65df0" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306163 1034 "4" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306163 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306163 1111 #uuid "d68b4eca-f9a8-5f56-a553-5d20378e9e19" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306164 1034 "18" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306164 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306164 1111 #uuid "213a9033-3c23-597e-a74b-6bf809abfba0" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306165 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306166 13194139534731 true] [17592186306165 1089 "q-29739" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306165 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534731 true] [17592186306165 1100 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x<1 \\\\ 6-5x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306165 1113 #uuid "f11a9469-77dd-5592-a5e2-e400aca2dd52" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306165 1145 "By the additive property of the definite integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}(6-5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{1}+{(6x-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2})|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}-\\frac{(0)^{3}}{3}]+(6(2)-\\frac{5(2)^{2}}{2}-6(1)+\\frac{5(1)^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{3}]+[12-10-6+\\frac{5}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306167 13194139534731 true] [17592186306166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306168 13194139534731 true] [17592186306166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306169 13194139534731 true] [17592186306166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306170 13194139534731 true] [17592186306166 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306171 13194139534731 true] [17592186306166 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534731 true] [17592186306166 1041 "selection" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306166 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306170 13194139534731 true] [17592186306166 1110 #uuid "721224c0-794a-527e-b70b-41b87069c5fc" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306167 1034 "-\\frac{6}{7}" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306167 1111 #uuid "ecbd94e8-0162-5a46-afb8-a1f117644bb2" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306168 1034 "\\frac{6}{7}" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306168 1111 #uuid "2c5c4ebe-ba72-5b6f-9790-2efac1eb642a" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306169 1034 "\\frac{7}{6}" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306169 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306169 1111 #uuid "62743952-0366-51bc-aeca-cea3b44cf55d" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306170 1034 "-\\frac{7}{6}" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306170 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306170 1111 #uuid "7d112a57-0263-575c-ad33-496e425ab1a0" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306171 1034 "\\frac{1}{7}" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306171 1111 #uuid "baba40d7-0ad2-5dde-9ccf-7de61d5c9aad" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306172 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306173 13194139534731 true] [17592186306172 1089 "q-316122" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306172 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534731 true] [17592186306172 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/64ce1ae5f198ce41e55414d853b1bd99e73d747cddb80412b30246bfdb13a907.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534731 true] [17592186306172 1113 #uuid "fa1ed858-bd59-5697-b4fd-680cbb34c192" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306172 1145 "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-3,1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & {\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & \\,{{{\\int }_{-3}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}\\,+\\,{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (1,4].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-3}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\, & =\\,{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\,8+6 \\\\  & =\\,14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306174 13194139534731 true] [17592186306173 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306175 13194139534731 true] [17592186306173 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534731 true] [17592186306173 1041 "field-1" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306173 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306175 13194139534731 true] [17592186306173 1110 #uuid "c4ea03e0-d23c-564a-ad66-b50e33e59bf0" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306174 1034 "28" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306174 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306174 1111 #uuid "bd73cb7b-9901-5d6e-bba2-9419023d3ec0" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306175 1034 "14" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306175 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306175 1111 #uuid "ac7443a1-00ce-54d3-9c5c-554d130cc1ae" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306176 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306177 13194139534731 true] [17592186306176 1089 "q-338319" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306176 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534731 true] [17592186306176 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/82096496503c058ae31473029f7844ddff99fad024dd786c7bad88985279a9af.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of two line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534731 true] [17592186306176 1113 #uuid "c7b9963d-ecf3-5165-a89b-7d1a2c7f953c" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306176 1145 "The graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-1)$ and the line segment for $x\\in (-1,2].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & {\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+{\\int }_{-1}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,= & \\,{{{\\int }_{-5}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}\\,+\\,{{{\\int }_{-1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-1).$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\, & =\\,{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =\\,6+7.5 \\\\  & =\\,13.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306177 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306178 13194139534731 true] [17592186306177 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534731 true] [17592186306177 1041 "field-1" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306177 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306178 13194139534731 true] [17592186306177 1110 #uuid "9ad7120e-6272-51a0-92d0-34cc606a28d6" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306178 1034 "\\frac{27}{2}" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306178 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534731 true] [17592186306178 1111 #uuid "a97442ad-2e34-5024-b4fb-63ff1e470729" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306179 1017 #edb/ref 17592186306180 13194139534731 true] [17592186306179 1089 "q-338505" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306179 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534731 true] [17592186306179 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040192/assets/a1a419a984000b3b8559a19809416bcccc9eb8341985994c621c3a48d15109da.png)\n\nThe graph $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Then,\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534731 true] [17592186306179 1113 #uuid "0dd9a4c2-9470-5512-b36a-e625f617bf1b" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306179 1145 "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-4],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-3),$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,0],$ and the line segment for $x\\in (0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{4}}.\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-3).$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,0].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (0,4].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =bh \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}+{A}_{4} \\\\  & =1-1+\\frac{9}{2}+4 \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306181 13194139534731 true] [17592186306180 1000 #edb/ref 17592186306182 13194139534731 true] [17592186306180 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534731 true] [17592186306180 1041 "field-1" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306180 1104 #edb/ref 17592186306182 13194139534731 true] [17592186306180 1110 #uuid "60ec4486-a34c-5ab2-abb2-f87ce2c7f28c" 13194139534731 true] [17592186306181 1034 "\\frac{21}{2}" 13194139534731 true] 